1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,231
In the pilot, the author opened a pull
request.

2
00:00:03,411 --> 00:00:05,640
Here, you are the teammate reviewing it.

3
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:11,045
Open the approved terminal with your own
authorized account and confirm that the

4
00:00:11,045 --> 00:00:14,833
GitHub command line is pointed at the
training repository.

5
00:00:15,013 --> 00:00:17,959
This example uses pull request forty-two.

6
00:00:18,139 --> 00:00:22,573
We will inspect the description, the
actual patch, and the required checks

7
00:00:22,573 --> 00:00:24,731
before you record your own decision.

8
00:00:26,173 --> 00:00:29,768
Run g h p r view for pull request forty-
two.

9
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:33,786
Read what the author says they changed and
which issue it closes.

10
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:38,917
Here the business rule is that quantities
must be positive, and the public function

11
00:00:38,917 --> 00:00:40,408
keeps the same signature.

12
00:00:40,588 --> 00:00:42,459
Note the claimed test result.

13
00:00:42,639 --> 00:00:47,692
When you read the next command's output,
use the issue and its acceptance criteria

14
00:00:47,692 --> 00:00:48,739
as the reference.

15
00:00:50,215 --> 00:00:52,124
Run g h p r diff.

16
00:00:52,304 --> 00:00:56,624
The patch should show one changed
comparison in the validator and one

17
00:00:56,624 --> 00:00:57,626
regression test.

18
00:00:57,806 --> 00:01:02,281
Read whether the assertion would have
failed before the fix, and check that it

19
00:01:02,281 --> 00:01:04,002
exercises the public function.

20
00:01:04,182 --> 00:01:08,160
If you see unrelated edits, follow up on
why they are present.

21
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:12,865
The author's summary helps you navigate,
while the diff is your evidence for what

22
00:01:12,865 --> 00:01:13,312
changed.

23
00:01:14,757 --> 00:01:20,473
Use g h p r checks with the required flag
to focus on the checks configured as

24
00:01:20,473 --> 00:01:22,745
required for this pull request.

25
00:01:22,925 --> 00:01:26,862
Open the relevant run when you need to
know what actually executed.

26
00:01:27,042 --> 00:01:29,752
If the check is pending, wait for the
result.

27
00:01:29,932 --> 00:01:34,370
A failing check or missing expected
evidence needs investigation.

28
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:39,349
If the author pushes new commits, refresh
the diff and checks before deciding.

29
00:01:40,798 --> 00:01:42,648
Try the alternate scenario.

30
00:01:42,828 --> 00:01:47,884
The check is green, but the contributed
test never calls the validator with zero.

31
00:01:48,064 --> 00:01:52,332
In this case you can request changes and
explain the missing assertion.

32
00:01:52,512 --> 00:01:57,290
A precise review tells the author what
observable behavior to verify.

33
00:01:57,470 --> 00:02:00,320
This is one possible decision for the
reviewer.

34
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,770
Use it when the change needs work, and
wait for the next revision before

35
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:05,482
reassessing.

36
00:02:06,923 --> 00:02:11,739
Return to the example where the diff
matches the issue and the tests cover all

37
00:02:11,739 --> 00:02:12,479
four inputs.

38
00:02:12,659 --> 00:02:17,764
If that evidence supports your approval,
submit it with g h p r review and a

39
00:02:17,764 --> 00:02:18,771
concise reason.

40
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:21,163
The human reviewer takes this action.

41
00:02:21,343 --> 00:02:25,929
After submitting, confirm that the review
was recorded for the pull request.

42
00:02:26,109 --> 00:02:29,673
Your approval indicates what you verified
at this revision.

43
00:02:31,132 --> 00:02:35,791
Pair with a teammate and alternate author
and reviewer roles on the training

44
00:02:35,791 --> 00:02:36,466
repository.

45
00:02:36,646 --> 00:02:41,445
The reviewer should be able to state the
issue, identify the changed behavior,

46
00:02:41,445 --> 00:02:45,753
point to the relevant check, and explain
the decision in one sentence.

47
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:49,775
Change only the evidence in a second round
and decide again.

48
00:02:49,955 --> 00:02:54,488
This gives you practice recognizing when
the same looking pull request should

49
00:02:54,488 --> 00:02:56,077
receive a different review.
